{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Bounded-loop reproduction evidence on a real 3.11 venv\n\nThe completion verifier returned FAIL with 5 of 8 criteria unmet, all of them about\nreproduction evidence that had never been captured. It is right that it was missing. Here it\nis, measured rather than asserted, including the part that does not flatter the fix.\n\n### The loop, and its exact command\n\nInterpreter: `Python 3.11.16` (`.rebar/scratch/py311-repro-43727/venv-3.11.16`), the same\npatch release the crashing CI cell runs. Both arms ran under xdist to stay faithful to CI's\nexecution shape — the failure signature is a *worker* crash, so a non-xdist run cannot\nproduce it at all.\n\n    PYTHONPATH=\"$tree/src\" python -m pytest \\\n      tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py -n 4 -p no:randomly -q\n\nEach iteration was wall-clock bounded at 90s so a hang would be counted rather than waited\non; rc 124/142 was classified as a hang, and worker-crash text was matched separately.\n\n| Arm | Tree | Iterations | pass | crash | hang | other |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PRE-FIX | `e84e4c5e943` (parent of the fix) | 40 | **40** | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| POST-FIX | `641531e62e5` (the merged fix) | 40 | **40** | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n\n### What this establishes, and what it does not\n\n**Reproduction rate on this host is 0/40 pre-fix.** Combined with the earlier 0/100 across\n3.11.16 / 3.11.15 / 3.12.14, that is 0 reproductions in 140 local attempts against code that\n*provably crashed in CI*. So the honest conclusion is not \"the fix works\" — it is that **this\nhost cannot reproduce the defect at all**, in either arm, and therefore the local loop has no\ndiscriminating power between them.\n\nThat matters more than a green post-fix column would. A post-fix 40/40 taken alone would\nlook like proof and would be worthless, because the pre-fix arm is *also* 40/40. Recording\nonly the post-fix number would have manufactured false confidence. The pre-fix arm is the\ncontrol that shows the local experiment is null.\n\n### Criterion 2 — the `cancelling`-at-`queue.get()` state\n\nIt cannot be obtained, and I am stating that rather than leaving it open. The criterion asks\nwhether the worker task is parked at `queue.get()` in a `cancelling` state, to separate a\nreal cancellation-consumption defect from scheduling latency. Capturing that requires a live\nreproduction to inspect. Across 140 local attempts there was none, so the discriminating\nobservation was never available to make. This is the second branch of the verifier's own\nremediation for that criterion.\n\n### Where the discriminating evidence actually lives: CI\n\nThe defect is CI-only, so the differential has to be run there — and it is running now, as a\nnatural experiment rather than a contrived one:\n\n| Patchset | Parent | Contains the fix? | py3.11 cell |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3008 ps1 (`f2200580a17`) | `e84e4c5e943` | no | **CRASHED** — run [35510440501](https://github.com/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/35510440501), `worker 'gw3' crashed while running tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py::test_worker_cancellation_survives_job_completion_race`, 1 failed / 20443 passed |\n| 3008 ps2 (`db06c1257a9`) | `641531e62e5` | yes | run [35517603759](https://github.com/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/35517603759), in progress |\n\nSame change, same test, same matrix cell; the only difference between the two arms is\nwhether the fix is in the parent. That is the attribution the third criterion asks for, and\nit will be conclusive in the direction CI reports — including if it reports that the crash\nrecurs, which would mean the teardown join was not the trigger.\n\n### Trigger attribution, stated plainly\n\nNot yet attributed to an interpreter patch release. The 3.11.16 / 3.11.15 / 3.12.14 sweep\nwas inconclusive because none of them reproduced it. The live hypothesis is **test-side\nscheduling latency under CI's load** — a runner under heavier contention than this host\nwidens the window in which the unbounded teardown join can park forever, and an unbounded\njoin is the only mechanism present that converts a widened window into a dead worker rather\nthan a slow test. The local nulls are consistent with that (an unloaded host never widens the\nwindow), but consistency is not proof, and I am not claiming it as one. Run 35517603759 is\nthe test.\n\nArtifacts cleaned up after measurement; the pre-fix tree was a throwaway detached worktree at\n`e84e4c5e943`.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789916989751871142,"uuid":"4c92b0c5-756b-483f-bb94-683e15fad51b"}